</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:liyiming.tech@gmail.com">liyiming.tech@gmail.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">© none</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large web-scale datasets have driven the rapid advancement of pre-trained language models (PLMs), but unauthorized data usage has raised serious copyright concerns. Existing dataset ownership verification (DOV) methods typically assume that watermarks remain stable during inference; however, this assumption often fails under natural noise and adversary-crafted perturbations. We propose the first certified dataset ownership verification method for PLMs based on dual-space smoothing (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, DSSmoothing). To address the challenges of text discreteness and semantic sensitivity, DSSmoothing introduces continuous perturbations in the embedding space to capture semantic robustness and applies controlled token reordering in the permutation space to capture sequential robustness. DSSmoothing consists of two stages: in the first stage, triggers are collaboratively embedded in both spaces to generate norm-constrained and robust watermarked datasets; in the second stage, randomized smoothing is applied in both spaces during verification to compute the watermark robustness (WR) of suspicious models and statistically compare it with the principal probability (PP) values of a set of benign models. Theoretically, DSSmoothing provides provable robustness guarantees for dataset ownership verification by ensuring that WR consistently exceeds PP under bounded dual-space perturbations. Extensive experiments on multiple representative web datasets demonstrate that DSSmoothing achieves stable and reliable verification performance and exhibits robustness against potential adaptive attacks.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Certified Dataset Watermarking, Dataset Ownership Verification, Pre-trained Language Models, AI Copyright Protection, Data Protection
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, large web-scale datasets have become a critical factor in advancing the performance of pre-trained language models (PLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These datasets consist of high-quality data systematically collected from diverse web sources, ranging from professionally curated news articles to user-generated reviews and social media posts. PLMs trained on such datasets exhibit remarkable abilities in language understanding, reasoning, and text generation, and have been widely deployed in various natural language processing applications, including text summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">El-Kassas et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and sentiment analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasr et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15303v1/introduction2.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/75;" width="452" height="75" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Comparison between existing PLMs DOV methods and our proposed DSSmoothing. Existing DOV approaches are highly sensitive to natural noise and adversary-crafted perturbations, leading to degraded confidence and semantic corruption. In contrast, DSSmoothing applies dual-space smoothing in both embedding and permutation spaces to preserve semantics and enhance robustness, enabling certified and reliable ownership verification under bounded perturbations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the rapid development of PLMs has raised growing concerns about unauthorized data usage. Many of the datasets involved are raw and uncurated web collections directly obtained from online platforms, rather than well-licensed or packaged datasets. Recent disputes have underscored this issue: the New York Times sued OpenAI and Microsoft in December 2023 for using its articles in training<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://smithhopen.com/2025/07/17/nyt-v-openai-microsoft-ai-copyright-lawsuit-update-2025/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://smithhopen.com/2025/07/17/nyt-v-openai-microsoft-ai-copyright-lawsuit-update-2025/</a></span></span></span>
, while platforms such as Reddit<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://www.theregister.com/2023/04/18/reddit_charging_ai_api/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.theregister.com/2023/04/18/reddit_charging_ai_api/</a></span></span></span>
and Twitter<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2023/04/19/musk-threatens-to-sue-microsoft-over-twitter-data-being-used-in-ai.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.cnbc.com/2023/04/19/musk-threatens-to-sue-microsoft-over-twitter-data-being-used-in-ai.html</a></span></span></span> have restricted access to their raw data to prevent exploitation for AI training. Constructing such large-scale datasets requires enormous human and financial resources, yet their use is typically governed by strict licensing agreements, often limited to educational or research purposes. Therefore, preventing the unauthorized use of web-scale datasets has become an increasingly urgent and critical challenge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, dataset ownership verification (DOV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> serves as an effective post-hoc auditing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> method that determines whether a suspicious model has been trained on a protected dataset by examining its prediction behaviors on specially designed verification samples under black-box settings. The process generally involves two stages: dataset watermarking and ownership verification. In the first stage, dataset owners make small, deliberate modifications to a subset of samples to generate a watermarked version that preserves normal accuracy on benign tests while inducing distinctive, predefined behaviors on verification samples. In the second stage, defenders (<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, dataset owners) query the suspicious model through API access and check whether such behaviors occur. If detected, it can be concluded that the model was indeed trained on the protected dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we revisit existing text DOV methods. These methods typically assume that the verification process is reliable, <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, the word embeddings of watermarked samples remain unchanged during inference. However, this assumption often fails in practice, as a malicious model owner may deliberately inject subtle perturbations during prediction to circumvent auditing. Our experiments confirm that the performance of existing DOV methods degrades sharply under both natural noise and deliberately adversary-crafted perturbations, revealing their inherent vulnerability. To the best of our knowledge, there exists only one pioneering work, CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which has preliminarily explored certified dataset ownership verification in the image domain by introducing random noise at the sample level to ensure robustness against constrained pixel-level perturbations. This naturally raises a key question: <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we achieve certified watermarks for PLMs dataset ownership verification?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The answer to this question is affirmative, although certified watermarking methods from the image domain cannot be directly transferred to pre-trained language models (PLMs). The difficulty mainly lies in two aspects. First, this method relies on well-defined continuous perturbations to enable noise-based certification, whereas text consists of discrete tokens that cannot accommodate smooth Gaussian noise as pixels do. Second, small perturbations in images typically preserve visual content, while text is highly semantically sensitive, and even minor modifications may disrupt linguistic fluency or alter meaning. To address these challenges, we propose DSSmoothing, the first certified dataset watermarking method for PLMs based on dual-space smoothing (as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In general, our DSSmoothing operates in two stages. In the first stage, inspired by TextCRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, it collaboratively embeds triggers in both the embedding and permutation spaces to generate norm-constrained and robust watermarked datasets. This design jointly addresses the aforementioned challenges: in the embedding space, continuous perturbations are applied to map discrete tokens into continuous vector space, thereby capturing semantic robustness and re-enabling noise-based certification; in the permutation space, controlled token reordering is performed to enhance sequential robustness while maintaining linguistic fluency and semantic consistency. In the second stage, DSSmoothing applies smoothing perturbations in both spaces to obtain the watermark robustness (WR) of the suspicious model and the principal probabilities (PP) of a set of benign models. The computed WR and PP metrics support our theoretical analysis, which proves that under bounded dual-space perturbations, WR (defined on watermarked samples) remains strictly higher than PP (defined on clean samples). Therefore, when the WR of a suspicious model significantly exceeds the majority of PP values from benign models, we can reliably conclude that the model has been trained on the protected dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the main contributions of this paper are fourfold: <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1)</span> We revisit existing DOV methods for PLMs and reveal their vulnerability during inference, where natural noise and deliberately crafted adversarial perturbations can severely degrade verification performance; <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2)</span> We propose DSSmoothing, the first certified watermarking method for PLMs based on dual-space smoothing, which provides robustness guarantees for dataset ownership verification; <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3)</span> We provide a theoretical analysis that proves the certified robustness of DSSmoothing under bounded dual-space perturbations and clarifies the conditions under which this guarantee holds;
<span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4)</span> We conduct extensive experiments on multiple representative web datasets to validate the effectiveness of our method and its robustness against potential adaptive attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background <span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">&amp;</span> Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Dataset Auditing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dataset auditing (DA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite> aims to determine whether a dataset has been used to train a suspicious model, and can be categorized into non-intrusive and intrusive approaches. Non-intrusive methods leave the dataset unchanged but often suffer from high false-positive rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025d</a>)</cite>. Therefore, this paper focuses on intrusive approaches, particularly dataset ownership verification (DOV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. DOV introduces imperceptible watermark samples into the original dataset to construct a watermarked version, enabling ownership verification in a black-box setting. These samples preserve model performance on clean test data while inducing distinctive behaviors on verification inputs. Research has evolved from poisoned-label backdoors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to clean-label backdoors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and further to harmless watermarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, with extensions to point clouds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, text-to-image diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and speech data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. More recently, attention has shifted to pre-trained language models (PLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where textual backdoors remain the dominant strategy (see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1. Backdoor Attacks in PLMs ‣ Appendix B Related Works ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). However, existing DOV techniques lack theoretical robustness guarantees and are vulnerable to adaptive attacks. Recently, Qiao <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposed the first certified dataset watermarking (CertDW) method, achieving provable robustness under bounded perturbations in the image domain, as detailed in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Random Smoothing and CertDW ‣ 2. Background &amp; Related Works ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Yet, certified dataset ownership verification for textual data remains challenging due to discreteness and semantic sensitivity.
Motivated by this gap, this paper aims to develop a certified DOV method for textual data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Random Smoothing and CertDW</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Randomized Smoothing (RS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2019</a>)</cite> was originally introduced for adversarial defense in image classification. Its core idea is to add Gaussian noise to the input multiple times and aggregate predictions via majority voting, ensuring stable outputs within a bounded perturbation (see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2. Randomized Smoothing ‣ Appendix B Related Works ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>). Later studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2023</a>)</cite> extended RS to model watermarking by injecting noise into the parameter space, but these methods rely on training details and remain limited to model-level copyright protection. Building on this line of work, Qiao <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> proposed CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the first to apply RS to dataset ownership verification. CertDW provides robustness guarantees in the image sample space, showing that ownership verification remains reliable under malicious attacks as long as certain conditions are satisfied, as detailed below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/sst-2_badnet_sigma0.3.svg" id="S2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:109/84;" width="109" height="84"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SST-2 (BadWord)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/sst-2_addsent_sigma0.35.svg" id="S2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:109/84;" width="109" height="84"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SST-2 (AddSent)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>WSR of watermarked samples under perturbations in the embedding space. <math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the noise and adversary-crafted directions, respectively, and ‘<math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>’ marks the original watermarked sample. Left: BadWord; right: AddSent.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</span>
For a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-class classification task, the base classifier <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained on the protected watermarked dataset <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> with class-dependent perturbations <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Delta}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Delta}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the RS method, the smoothed verifier is defined as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\hat{\bm{x}}_{k},\sigma):=\arg\max_{y}\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{k}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\hat{\bm{x}}_{k},\sigma):=\arg\max_{y}\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(f_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{k}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{x}}_{k}=\bm{x}_{k}+\bm{\Delta}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{x}}_{k}=\bm{x}_{k}+\bm{\Delta}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>. To capture the prediction stability of benign models under noise, the <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">principal probability (PP)</span> is defined as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})=\big\|\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}p(\bm{x}_{k}\mid g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})\big\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})=\big\|\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}p(\bm{x}_{k}\mid g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})\big\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{y}(\bm{x}\mid g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})=\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(\arg\max g(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon};w)=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{y}(\bm{x}\mid g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})=\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(\arg\max g(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon};w)=y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which in practice is estimated via Monte Carlo sampling. To measure watermark persistence, the <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">watermark robustness (WR)</span> is defined as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(f_{\theta},\bm{\epsilon})=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(f_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{k}+\bm{\Delta}_{k}+\bm{\epsilon})=\hat{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(f_{\theta},\bm{\epsilon})=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}\mathbb{P}_{\bm{\epsilon}}(f_{\theta}(\bm{x}_{k}+\bm{\Delta}_{k}+\bm{\epsilon})=\hat{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is the target watermark label. The maximum watermark perturbation magnitude during training is given by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\max_{k=1,\dots,K}\|\bm{\Delta}_{k}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\max_{k=1,\dots,K}\|\bm{\Delta}_{k}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. From <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> benign models, a set of PP values <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{C}^{1},\dots,P_{C}^{J}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>J</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{C}^{1},\dots,P_{C}^{J}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained and sorted as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{P_{C}^{(1)}\leq\cdots\leq P_{C}^{(J)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{C}^{(1)}\leq\cdots\leq P_{C}^{(J)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; with allowance for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> outliers and significance level <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the calibration threshold is then determined by conformal prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vovk, 2012</a>)</cite> as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, dataset ownership verification is guaranteed if and only if: <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(f_{\theta},\bm{\epsilon})&gt;\Phi\left(\frac{r}{\sigma}\right)+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(f_{\theta},\bm{\epsilon})&gt;\Phi\left(\frac{r}{\sigma}\right)+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\bm{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the CDF of the standard Gaussian distribution. However, directly extending this formalization to text data is non-trivial:
text tokens cannot accommodate continuous Gaussian noise, making the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> norm bounds meaningless for word-level or sentence-level watermarks; moreover, unlike pixel perturbations that preserve visual content, text modifications must maintain linguistic fluency and semantic coherence. These fundamental differences necessitate a new certification mechanism tailored to the discrete and semantically sensitive characteristics of textual data, which is the focus of this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Revisiting PLM Dataset Watermarking</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing PLMs dataset watermarking methods typically assume that the verification process is <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reliable</span>, with embedded representations remaining unaltered. However, in real-world applications, embeddings are likely to encounter two types of perturbations: natural noise arising from normal operations (<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, transmission interference) and adversary-crafted perturbations intentionally designed to evade ownership verification while preserving semantic meaning. In this section, we systematically analyze the vulnerabilities of existing text DOV methods under these two perturbation scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_badnet1.svg" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:105/53;" width="105" height="53"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">BadWord</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_addsent1.svg" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:105/54;" width="105" height="54"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">AddSent</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>WSR of watermarked samples under noise.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Vulnerability to Stochastic Noise</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Settings.</span>
We hereby adopt BadWord <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and AddSent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>)</cite> as the representative watermarking techniques on the AG’s News <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2015</a>)</cite> and SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Socher et al., 2013</a>)</cite> datasets for discussion. They serve as representatives of word-level and sentence-level backdoor watermarks, respectively. Following the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, we set the target label <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> to 1 and the watermarking rate to 10% on both datasets. Specifically, BadWord employs the trigger words {“cf”, “mn”} inserted at random positions, while AddSent appends the trigger sentence “I watch this 3D movie.” For classification, we use fine-tuned BERT-base models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. To simulate stochastic noise that may arise during normal operations in web-scale deployments, we inject Gaussian noise <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math> into the word embeddings during inference, with noise magnitude <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3. Revisiting PLM Dataset Watermarking ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the watermark success rates (WSR) of both methods decrease significantly as the noise magnitude increases. In particular, on the AG News dataset, even a small stochastic perturbation with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0.066" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.066</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0.066</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the WSR of AddSent from 100% to below 40%. These results demonstrate that the PLMs dataset watermarks are highly sensitive to naturally occurring stochastic perturbations during normal operations, thereby revealing a fundamental vulnerability in existing watermarking methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Vulnerability under Adversary-Crafted Perturbations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Settings.</span>
To analyze the effect of adversary-crafted perturbations on watermarks, we visualize the watermark success rate (WSR) in a two-dimensional embedding subspace defined by the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural noise direction</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adversary-crafted perturbation direction</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}=\operatorname{sign}(\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}=\operatorname{sign}(\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I))</annotation></semantics></math> represents natural stochastic noise, while <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}=\operatorname{sign}(\nabla_{\bm{e}}\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta},\bm{e},y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒆</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}=\operatorname{sign}(\nabla_{\bm{e}}\mathcal{L}(\bm{\theta},\bm{e},y))</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the adversarial direction that suppresses watermark activation, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is the word embedding.
We perturb the original watermarked embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> along these two directions and measure the resulting WSR in the surrounding region. The perturbed embedding space is defined as
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{E}}={\hat{\bm{e}}+\varepsilon_{N}d_{N}+\varepsilon_{A}d_{A}\mid\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{A}\in\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{E}}={\hat{\bm{e}}+\varepsilon_{N}d_{N}+\varepsilon_{A}d_{A}\mid\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{A}\in\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon_{N},\varepsilon_{A})</annotation></semantics></math> control the perturbation magnitudes. The original sample <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{e}}</annotation></semantics></math> (black dot at the origin) serves as the reference. Finally, we examine how WSR varies across this subspace to illustrate watermark robustness under both natural and adversary-crafted perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2. Random Smoothing and CertDW ‣ 2. Background &amp; Related Works ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, adversary-crafted perturbations severely degrade watermark preservation, far exceeding the effect of stochastic noise. For instance, on the SST-2 dataset with AddSent, small perturbations along <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> can reduce the WSR from nearly 100% to almost 0%. Similar trends are observed on the AG dataset (see Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Additional Results on the AG News Dataset ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). These results highlight that adversary-crafted perturbations pose a far more fundamental threat to PLM dataset watermarks than naturally occurring noise.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/MAIN1.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/140;" width="452" height="140"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The main pipeline of our DSSmoothing for certified PLMs dataset ownership verification.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Proposed Mrthod</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As demonstrated in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Revisiting PLM Dataset Watermarking ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, existing PLMs dataset watermarking methods are highly vulnerable to both natural noise and adversary-crafted perturbations, making them easy to evade. These observations motivate us to design a certified DOV method (<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, DSSmoothing) for PLMs that provides provable robustness against such perturbations. The details are presented in this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we first review the main pipeline of text dataset ownership verification (DOV) methods, and then introduce the key definition and lemma that form the theoretical foundation of our proposed DSSmoothing method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Pipeline of Text Classification DOV Methods.</span>
Consider a standard text classification dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_{n},y_{n})\}_{n=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes, where each text sequence <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{n}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{n}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a label <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}\in\mathcal{Y}=\{1,2,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}\in\mathcal{Y}=\{1,2,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>. During the watermarking phase, the dataset owner constructs a watermarked dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{w}=\mathcal{D}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{w}=\mathcal{D}_{m}\cup\mathcal{D}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> contains modified samples from a small selected subset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{s}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{s}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the remaining benign samples. The modified subset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated through a dataset-specific text generator <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{X}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{X}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and a label generator <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{Y}:\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{Y}:\mathcal{Y}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}=\{(\hat{\bm{x}},\hat{y}):\hat{\bm{x}}=G_{X}(\bm{x}),\hat{y}=G_{Y}(y),(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}=\{(\hat{\bm{x}},\hat{y}):\hat{\bm{x}}=G_{X}(\bm{x}),\hat{y}=G_{Y}(y),(\bm{x},y)\in\mathcal{D}_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, in BadWord-based DOV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> inserts specific trigger words (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, “cf”, “mn”) into the text,
and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{Y}(y)=\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{Y}(y)=\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns a fixed target label. In AddSent-based DOV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> appends a trigger sentence (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, “I watch this 3D movie”) to the original text. The watermarking rate is defined as
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\triangleq\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{m}|}{|\mathcal{D}_{w}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≜</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\triangleq\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{m}|}{|\mathcal{D}_{w}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. During the ownership verification phase, the dataset owner verifies whether a suspicious model <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot;\bm{\theta}):\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot;\bm{\theta}):\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>
has been trained on the protected dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>
by querying it with specially designed verification samples in a black-box setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, unlike conventional DOV pipelines that operate solely in the token space, we model watermarking operations within a dual-space representation that integrates the embedding and permutation spaces. This design unifies semantic and sequential perturbations and serves as the foundation for the subsequent certified robustness analysis. The formal definition and corresponding robustness lemma are given below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0 (Embedding-Permutation Dual-Space Representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given input text sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=[t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=[t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> of fixed length <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the dual-space representation as a tuple <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{U},\bm{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{U},\bm{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">embedding space</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the embedding matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}=[\bm{w}_{1},\ldots,\bm{w}_{n}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}=[\bm{w}_{1},\ldots,\bm{w}_{n}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> being the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding vector of token <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">permutation space</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\subseteq\{0,1\}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\subseteq\{0,1\}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the permutation matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}=[\bm{u}_{1},\ldots,\bm{u}_{n}]^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}=[\bm{u}_{1},\ldots,\bm{u}_{n}]^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard basis vector and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{u}_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒖</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{u}_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates mapping the token at position <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to position <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (with exactly one 1 per row and column). The complete text representation is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{E}=\bm{U}\cdot\bm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{E}=\bm{U}\cdot\bm{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 0 (Certified Robustness in Dual-Space Randomized Smoothing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{U},\bm{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{U},\bm{W})</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual-space representation (see Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 0 (Embedding-Permutation Dual-Space Representation ( , )). ‣ 4.1. Preliminaries ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>),
with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> a permutation matrix and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> an embedding matrix, and let
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be a base classifier.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{T}_{p}\to\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\mathcal{U}\times\mathcal{T}_{p}\to\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> be a permutation transformation and
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{W}\times\mathcal{T}_{e}\to\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{W}\times\mathcal{T}_{e}\to\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding transformation, and assume
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math> (uniform-based permutation) and
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜺</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math> (Gaussian-based embedding),
where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity matrix of appropriate dimension; assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent. Define the smoothed classifier</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathbb{P}_{(\bm{\rho},\bm{\varepsilon})}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathbb{P}_{(\bm{\rho},\bm{\varepsilon})}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{A}=\mathbb{P}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y_{A}\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{A}=\mathbb{P}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y_{A}\big]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{B}=\max_{y\neq y_{A}}\mathbb{P}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{B}=\max_{y\neq y_{A}}\mathbb{P}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot\phi(\bm{W},\bm{\varepsilon}))=y\big]</annotation></semantics></math>,
and suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{A}&gt;p_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{A}&gt;p_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant for any perturbations satisfying <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bm{\delta}_{e}\|_{2}&lt;r_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{\delta}_{e}\|_{2}&lt;r_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bm{\delta}_{p}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{\delta}_{p}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}=\tfrac{\sigma}{2}\big(\Phi^{-1}(p_{A})-\Phi^{-1}(p_{B})\big),\qquad r_{p}=\lambda\,(p_{A}-p_{B})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}=\tfrac{\sigma}{2}\big(\Phi^{-1}(p_{A})-\Phi^{-1}(p_{B})\big),\qquad r_{p}=\lambda\,(p_{A}-p_{B}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Threat Model.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we adopt the classical DOV setting, where the dataset owner embeds trigger-based watermarks into the text before release, and downstream users may train PLMs on it. During the verification phase, the owner can only query the suspicious model in a black-box setting. Unlike prior work that implicitly assumes watermark embeddings remain stable during verification, we explicitly consider two realistic perturbations: stochastic noise that naturally arises during inference, and worst-case perturbations applied to word embeddings to evade ownership verification while preserving semantics. This threat model better reflects web-scale deployment environments, thereby providing a more reliable basis for evaluating verification effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Goal of Certified Text Dataset Watermark.</span>
The primary goal is to provide provable verification guarantees for text dataset ownership, ensuring that the watermark remains effective even under removal or interference. Specifically, we require two robustness conditions to hold: the trigger should remain reliably activated under inference-time noise, and under bounded embedding perturbations, even if an adversary slightly modifies or completely removes the trigger, the model should still recognize the verification text through its learned semantic “memory.” Together, these conditions ensure the effectiveness of ownership verification. In practice, we certify a watermark once the minimum of these two metrics exceeds a predefined threshold; this threshold can be interpreted as a worst-case lower bound on the success probability, thereby providing provable robustness for text dataset ownership.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Overview of the Proposed Method</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2. Vulnerability under Adversary-Crafted Perturbations ‣ 3. Revisiting PLM Dataset Watermarking ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, our DSSmoothing consists of two main stages: <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> dataset watermarking and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> ownership verification. In the first stage, we control perturbation strength by optimizing trigger embeddings in the embedding space and adjust word order through group-based reordering in the permutation space, thereby producing a watermarked dataset that meets certified radius constraints. In the second stage, we compute the watermark robustness (WR) of the suspicious model under dual-space smoothing, construct a calibration set from the principal probability (PP) values of benign models, and perform ownership verification using conformal prediction. If the WR value exceeds most PP values in the calibration set, the suspicious model is regarded as trained on the protected dataset. The technical details are as follows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Dataset Watermarking of DSSmoothing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this stage, we generate the watermarked dataset by embedding triggers jointly in the embedding and permutation spaces. Inspired by TextCRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the embedding space employs local pooling and iterative scaling to precisely control perturbations, whereas the permutation space utilizes group-based reordering to introduce subtle local word-order variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermark Optimization in Embedding Space.</span>
To precisely control trigger perturbations within the certified radius <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, we insert triggers at predefined positions <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and employ a local pooling strategy to carefully quantify their influence on the resulting sentence representations:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{l}^{(i)}=\frac{\sum_{j=p_{i}-u}^{p_{i}+u}\bm{w}_{j}m_{j}}{\sum_{j=p_{i}-u}^{p_{i}+u}m_{j}},\quad h_{s}=\tfrac{1}{|P|}\sum_{i=1}^{|P|}h_{l}^{(i)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{l}^{(i)}=\frac{\sum_{j=p_{i}-u}^{p_{i}+u}\bm{w}_{j}m_{j}}{\sum_{j=p_{i}-u}^{p_{i}+u}m_{j}},\quad h_{s}=\tfrac{1}{|P|}\sum_{i=1}^{|P|}h_{l}^{(i)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{j}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{j}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the word embedding at position <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the attention mask, and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is an adaptive window size based on sequence length <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the sentence embedding after trigger insertion.
We then constrain its deviation from the original representation <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{s}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{s}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a maximum perturbation <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> (corresponding to <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a safety factor <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\!\in\!(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\!\in\!(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the target perturbation <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}=\varepsilon_{\max}\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}=\varepsilon_{\max}\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The scaling factor is iteratively updated according to the following rule:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(t+1)}=\alpha^{(t)}\!\cdot\!\frac{\varepsilon_{t}}{\|h_{s}^{(t)}-h_{s}^{(0)}\|_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(t+1)}=\alpha^{(t)}\!\cdot\!\frac{\varepsilon_{t}}{\|h_{s}^{(t)}-h_{s}^{(0)}\|_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">until it converges to the optimal <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to the base trigger embedding <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{t}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{t}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the optimized trigger <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{w}_{t}^{\prime}=\alpha^{*}\bm{w}_{t}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{w}_{t}^{\prime}=\alpha^{*}\bm{w}_{t}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we construct the watermarked embedding matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{W}^{\prime}=\phi(\bm{W},\Delta_{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{W}^{\prime}=\phi(\bm{W},\Delta_{e})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{e}=\{(p,\bm{w}_{t}^{\prime}):p\!\in\!P\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{e}=\{(p,\bm{w}_{t}^{\prime}):p\!\in\!P\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents optimized trigger embeddings inserted at positions in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, with the perturbation guaranteed to satisfy <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{e}=\|\bm{W}^{\prime}-\bm{W}\|_{F}&lt;r_{\bm{e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝒆</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{e}=\|\bm{W}^{\prime}-\bm{W}\|_{F}&lt;r_{\bm{e}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Watermark Embedding in Permutation Space.</span>
To model the trigger-induced word-order variations, we adjust the permutation matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}</annotation></semantics></math> under the certified radius <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
While Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0 (Certified Robustness in Dual-Space Randomized Smoothing ( , )). ‣ 4.1. Preliminaries ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> formalizes this as uniform noise, we implement it via a group-based reordering scheme.
Specifically, the sequence is divided into groups of size <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and within each group <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{i}=\{i\lambda,\dots,\min((i+1)\lambda-1,n)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{i}=\{i\lambda,\dots,\min((i+1)\lambda-1,n)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, a random permutation <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}:\mathcal{I}_{i}\!\to\!\mathcal{I}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}:\mathcal{I}_{i}\!\to\!\mathcal{I}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied.
This yields the watermarked permutation matrix <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}^{\prime}=\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}^{\prime}=\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}\!\sim\!\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}\!\sim\!\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\delta}_{p}=\|\bm{U}^{\prime}-\bm{U}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑼</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\delta}_{p}=\|\bm{U}^{\prime}-\bm{U}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls perturbation strength: smaller <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> ensures finer local reordering, while <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\!=\!n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\!=\!n</annotation></semantics></math> produces full randomization.
Restricting permutations to local windows preserves semantic coherence while enhancing watermark robustness against reordering attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this stage, we estimate the WR value by selecting the minimum (across classes) probability from the prediction distribution (PD) of the suspicious model for the target class under dual-space smoothing, where the PD characterizes model outputs over all classes when dual-space noise is applied to test samples. After obtaining the WR value, we introduce the principal probability (PP) as a complementary statistic and employ conformal prediction to perform ownership verification. While WR focuses on the minimum confidence of watermarked samples, PP measures the overall prediction stability of benign models under the same perturbations. By computing PP values across multiple benign models, we construct a calibration set that reflects the behavior of models trained on non-protected datasets. If a sufficient proportion of PP values in this set are smaller than the WR of the suspicious model, we determine that the model was trained on the protected dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Performance (%) of dataset watermarking (BA, WSR) and ownership verification (VSR, WCA) on AG’s News dataset (AG) with BERT, OPT-1.3B, and GPT-2 under three noise levels (0.01, 0.02, 0.03). Best verification results are in bold.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:519.1pt;height:233.7pt;vertical-align:-114.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-53.2pt,23.9pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
