<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6. </span>Theoretical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the theoretical analysis of our DSSmoothing-based ownership verification method proposed in Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.
The detailed proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking).</h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the permutation noise <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and the embedding noise <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> are independent, and let their joint distribution be
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}=\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]\times\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}=\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]\times\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> the watermark robustness (WR) of the suspicious model <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> under dual-space smoothing, as defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the embedding-space and permutation-space watermark perturbations, respectively, and define <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\bm{\delta}_{e}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝜹</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\bm{\delta}_{e}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum perturbation magnitudes in the two spaces. Then, text dataset ownership verification in the dual space is guaranteed whenever both of the following conditions are satisfied:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)&gt;\Phi\!\left(\frac{r_{e}}{\sigma}\right)+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)&gt;\Phi\!\left(\frac{r_{e}}{\sigma}\right)+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)&gt;\frac{r_{p}}{2\lambda}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}\big)&gt;\frac{r_{p}}{2\lambda}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho,\varepsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0 (Ownership Verification via Dual-Space Conformal Prediction). ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the standard Gaussian distribution.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In general, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> indicates that, given a fixed watermark perturbation magnitude, a larger watermark robustness (WR) leads to a higher likelihood of successful ownership verification in both embedding and permutation spaces. Conversely, for a fixed WR, watermarks with smaller perturbation magnitudes are more easily guaranteed to pass the verification. In particular, the essential differences between our method and existing robustness certification approaches for adversarial examples or backdoor defenses are further discussed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Comparison with Certified Adversarial and Backdoor Robustness ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Main Settings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset and Model Selections.</span>
We evaluate our method on two widely-used text classification datasets
that exemplify web-collected data: AG’s News (AG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, a news classification dataset aggregated from over 2,000 online news sources with four topic categories (World, Sports, Business, and Sci/Tech), representing professionally curated journalism content; and SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Socher et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, a binary sentiment classification dataset derived from movie reviews scraped from Rotten Tomatoes, exemplifying user-generated web content. To validate the generalizability across different architectures, we conduct experiments on three representative pre-trained language models: BERT-base-uncased <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, GPT-2 base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and OPT-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline Selection.</span>
In this paper, we compare our method with Gaussian-Only, which directly adapts the image-based CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to text using only Gaussian noise for smoothing, to highlight the limitations of cross-domain transfer. We also provide results of Vanilla, a conventional watermarking approach, and Independent, a model trained without watermarking, as references.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Effects of the number of watermarked models.</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_watermark1.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/95;" width="224" height="95"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>Effects of the proportion of filtered outliers.</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_beta1.svg" id="S5.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/95;" width="224" height="95"></object>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F8.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>The resistance to model fine-tuning.</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_finetuning1.svg" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/75;" width="224" height="75"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F8.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>The resistance to model pruning.</figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_pruning1.svg" id="S5.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/74;" width="224" height="74"></object>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>
Following the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our evaluation covers two aspects: dataset watermarking and ownership verification. For dataset watermarking, we use benign accuracy (BA) and watermark success rate (WSR), which measure the model’s accuracy on clean and watermarked test sets, respectively. For ownership verification, we adopt verification success rate (VSR) and watermark certification accuracy (WCA). VSR denotes the probability that clean samples are predicted as the target class by the watermarked model under dual-space smoothing (equivalent to the false positive rate on independent models), while WCA measures the proportion of watermarked samples that a watermarked model can reliably certify as the target class within a bounded certification radius. Higher VSR and WCA indicate better verification performance, and we monitor WSR in verification to evaluate watermark robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
For dataset watermarking, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we adopt the BadWord and AddSent methods, with <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations around 0.6 and 1.0, respectively. For dataset verification, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we use benign and independent models, and set the outlier filtering ratio <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> to 0.05 for AG and 0.2 for SST-2. Further details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Detailed Experimental Settings ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.15303v1/heatmap_SST2_VSR.png" id="S5.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:105/88;" width="105" height="88" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F9.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SST-2 (VSR)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.15303v1/heatmap_SST2_WCA.png" id="S5.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:105/88;" width="105" height="88" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F9.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SST-2 (WCA)</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>Performance on SST-2 with different benign (rows) and watermarked (columns) model architectures.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure">
