<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10. </span>WSR of our method’s watermarked samples under perturbations in the embedding space. <math id="S5.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the noise and adversary-crafted directions, respectively, and ‘<math id="S5.F10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math>’ marks the original watermarked sample. Left: BadWord; right: AddSent.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Tables <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our method demonstrates strong dataset utility. The watermark success rate (WSR) remains around 98%, while the drop in benign accuracy (BA) is kept within 2% and is below 1% in most cases, indicating effective preservation of normal functionality. In terms of verification performance, our approach clearly outperforms the baselines and remains stable under various noise perturbations: the WSR of BERT and OPT-1.3B stays above 95%, and GPT-2 also exceeds 50%, a level of robustness lacking in traditional methods. Furthermore, under independent model evaluation, the false positive rate (FPR, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, VSR) is well controlled, not exceeding 14% on AG and 10% on SST-2. These results confirm that our proposed mechanism is both accurate and reliable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hereby analyze the effect of two key hyper-parameters at a noise level of 0.02 for both AG and SST-2. More results are in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Effects of the Number of Benign Models ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of the Number of watermarked Models.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, VSR and WCA vary with the number of watermarked models. On AG, both methods remain at 80–100%, showing robustness. Results indicate that even with only 10–20 models, high detection performance is achievable, and adding more provides limited gains, suggesting that defenders can verify ownership with few models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effects of the Proportion of Filtered Outliers.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, watermarking methods maintain high VSR and WCA as the filtering ratio <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> increases, while the VSR of benign models rises sharply, from 20% to 70% on AG and from 10% to 40% on SST-2, indicating higher false positives. These results reveal a trade-off between detection and false positives, requiring careful choice of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Resistance to Potential Adaptive Attacks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method against two adaptive attacks: fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and model pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, fine-tuning only causes minor initial drops in VSR and WCA, which quickly stabilize at high levels. In Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, both metrics remain stable even under pruning rates close to 100%. These results demonstrate strong robustness of our method against fine-tuning and pruning attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Model-level Transferability</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Main Results ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> assume that the benign and watermarked models share the same architecture, which may not hold in practice. We therefore evaluate BERT, GPT-2 (“GPT”), and OPT-1.3B (“OPT”) on SST-2. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, our method remains effective across different architectures despite capacity differences, showing robustness without prior knowledge of model structures. Results on AG are given in Appendix <a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Model-level Transferability on AG Dataset ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span>On the Effectiveness of Our Method</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we adopt the same methodology as in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Vulnerability under Adversary-Crafted Perturbations ‣ 3. Revisiting PLM Dataset Watermarking ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to validate the effectiveness of our approach. Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.1. Main Settings ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the results on the SST-2 dataset, where watermarked samples maintain high WSR under natural noise and remain robust under worst-case perturbations. For instance, even under large perturbations, the WSR on SST-2 remains effective. Results on the AG News dataset exhibit similar trends and are provided in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Additional Effectiveness Results on the AG News Dataset ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>. This indicates that completely removing the watermark is significantly harder.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we revisited existing dataset ownership verification (DOV) methods for pre-trained language models (PLMs) and revealed their vulnerabilities under natural noise and deliberately adversary-crafted perturbations. To address these issues, we proposed DSSmoothing, the first certified dataset watermarking method for PLMs based on dual-space smoothing. By introducing controlled perturbations in both embedding and permutation spaces, DSSmoothing captures semantic and sequential robustness simultaneously. Our theoretical analysis proves that under bounded perturbations, a strict lower bound exists between watermark robustness (WR) and principal probability (PP), thereby enabling provable ownership verification. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets validate the effectiveness of DSSmoothing, showing that it not only outperforms existing methods but also resists potential adaptive attacks. These findings highlight DSSmoothing as a novel perspective for copyright protection of large-scale text datasets, offering a feasible and theoretically guaranteed solution for trustworthy dataset sharing and trading.</p>
</div>
</section>
