<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hereby provide the detailed proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
To establish the theorem, it suffices to verify the robustness guarantees in both the embedding and permutation spaces.
The embedding-space proof follows directly from CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
so we focus on deriving the corresponding result in the permutation space.
The proof proceeds by establishing a sequence of lemmas that progressively lead to the desired robustness condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 0 (Lipschitz Continuity of Smoothed Classifier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).</h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a uniform distribution noise <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math>, the smoothed classifier <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})=\mathbb{P}_{(\bm{\rho},\bm{\varepsilon})}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot W)\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})=\mathbb{P}_{(\bm{\rho},\bm{\varepsilon})}\!\big[h(\psi(\bm{U},\bm{\rho})\cdot W)\big]</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 0 (Lipschitz Continuity of Smoothed Classifier ( , )). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> established, we now derive the robustness condition for dataset ownership verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 0.</h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the permutation noise <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> the watermark robustness (WR) of the suspicious model <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> under permutation-space smoothing, as defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), which is <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous under the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> norm. Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the permutation-space watermark perturbations, and define
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum perturbation magnitude in this space. Then, text dataset ownership verification in the permutation space is guaranteed if</p>
<table id="A1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;L.r_{p}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;L.r_{p}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th smallest element of the calibration set
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> constructed from benign models <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0 (Ownership Verification via Dual-Space Conformal Prediction). ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0 (Ownership Verification via Dual-Space Conformal Prediction). ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, dataset ownership verification holds if the following condition is satisfied:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the watermark robustness in the permutation space is defined as: <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)=\min_{k=1,\ldots,K}\ \mathbb{P}_{\rho}\!\Big(\arg\max f\big(\psi(\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k},\rho).\bm{W}_{k};\bm{\theta}\big)=\hat{y}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)=\min_{k=1,\ldots,K}\ \mathbb{P}_{\rho}\!\Big(\arg\max f\big(\psi(\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k},\rho).\bm{W}_{k};\bm{\theta}\big)=\hat{y}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes watermarked samples. We further define the corresponding robustness measure on clean samples as: <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)=\min_{k=1,\ldots,K}\ \mathbb{P}_{\rho}\!\Big(\arg\max f\big(\psi(\bm{U}_{k},\rho).\bm{W}_{k};\bm{\theta}\big)=\hat{y}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)=\min_{k=1,\ldots,K}\ \mathbb{P}_{\rho}\!\Big(\arg\max f\big(\psi(\bm{U}_{k},\rho).\bm{W}_{k};\bm{\theta}\big)=\hat{y}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the perturbation is bounded by <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k}-\bm{U}_{k}\|_{1}=\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{\bm{U}}_{k}-\bm{U}_{k}\|_{1}=\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}&lt;r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big|W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)\big|\leq L\cdot r_{p}\;\Longrightarrow\;W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)\geq\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-L\cdot r_{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big|W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)\big|\leq L\cdot r_{p}\;\Longrightarrow\;W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)\geq\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-L\cdot r_{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">To ensure the condition in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E13" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), it is sufficient to require</span></p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-L\cdot r_{p}&gt;P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{W}\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)-L\cdot r_{p}&gt;P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which corresponds exactly to the robustness condition in Eq. (<a href="#A1.E12" title="In Lemma 0. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
Therefore, dataset ownership verification in the permutation space is guaranteed under the stated robustness bound.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_theorem">Lemma 0 (<span id="A1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Certified Watermark Robustness in Permutation Space</span>).</h6>
<div id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the permutation noise <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\sim\mathcal{U}[-\lambda,\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> the watermark robustness (WR) of the suspicious model <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> under permutation-space smoothing, as defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). Let <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the permutation-space watermark perturbations, and define
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}=\max_{k=1,\ldots,K}\|\delta_{p}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum perturbation magnitude in this space. Then, text dataset ownership verification in the permutation space is guaranteed if</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;\frac{r_{p}}{2\lambda}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.913em;" width="0.913em"><mi>W</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\!\big(f_{\bm{\theta}},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}\big)&gt;\frac{r_{p}}{2\lambda}+P_{C}^{(J-m-\lfloor\alpha_{0}(J-m+1)\rfloor)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}^{(j)}(g_{w},\mathcal{P}_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th smallest element of the calibration set
<math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}(\mathcal{P}_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> constructed from benign models <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 0 (Ownership Verification via Dual-Space Conformal Prediction). ‣ 4.5. Ownership Verification of DSSmoothing ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 0 (Certified Watermark Robustness in Permutation Space). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> follows directly from Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 0 (Lipschitz Continuity of Smoothed Classifier ( , )). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; combining it with the embedding-space result from CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span>
According to Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 0 (Lipschitz Continuity of Smoothed Classifier ( , )). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the uniform-based smoothed classifier
<math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}(\bm{U},\bm{W})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑼</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{U}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> norm.
Combining this lemma with Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 0. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and substituting
<math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\tfrac{1}{2\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\tfrac{1}{2\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> into Eq. (<a href="#A1.E12" title="In Lemma 0. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we obtain
Eq. (<a href="#A1.E16" title="In Lemma 0 (Certified Watermark Robustness in Permutation Space). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) in Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 0 (Certified Watermark Robustness in Permutation Space). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Together with the embedding-space result established in CertDW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>,
this completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4 (Robustness Condition of Certified Text Dataset Watermarking). ‣ 4.6. Theoretical Analysis ‣ 4. The Proposed Mrthod ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1. </span>Backdoor Attacks in PLMs</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text classification is a fundamental task in natural language processing. With the prevalence of pre-trained language models (PLMs), such as BERT and GPT series, fine-tuning on downstream datasets has become the dominant paradigm for adapting models to specific tasks. However, this paradigm also amplifies the security risks of deep learning models, with backdoor attacks emerging as a notable threat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>. Backdoor attacks inject triggers during training or fine-tuning, enabling the model to perform well on normal inputs but produce attacker-specified outputs once the triggers appear. In text scenarios, attackers must embed triggers while preserving semantic fluency, which is more challenging than pixel-level perturbations in the vision domain. Nevertheless, the strong modeling and transferability capabilities of PLMs make them particularly vulnerable to such attacks. Consequently, backdoor attacks not only pose severe security threats but also demonstrate unique potential for dataset copyright protection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Among existing approaches, data-poisoning attacks are particularly critical: by injecting triggers into training data and modifying labels, any PLM fine-tuned on the poisoned dataset will inevitably inherit the backdoor behaviors, thus enabling ownership verification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Typical forms include word-level attacks (BadWord <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kurita et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite>), which insert meaningless character triggers; sentence-level attacks (AddSent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>)</cite>), which append fixed sentences; and structure-level attacks (SynBkd <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>), which exploit specific syntactic structures. In this work, we focus on BadWord and AddSent methods to construct practical watermarking techniques for text dataset copyright protection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2. </span>Randomized Smoothing</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We here provide the formal definition of randomized smoothing (RS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, a certified defense technique that leverages Gaussian perturbations to provide provable robustness guarantees.
Specifically, for any input <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, a smoothed classifier <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by adding Gaussian noise <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the robustness level. This classifier outputs the class most frequently predicted by the base model <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> under noise perturbations: <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\bm{x}):=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\bm{x}):=\arg\max_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on the Neyman-Pearson Lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neyman and Pearson, 1933</a>)</cite>, Cohen <span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2019</a>)</cite> established the certification mechanism of this method under <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations. Let <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the most probable class under noise conditions (<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{A}=\arg\max_{y}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{A}=\arg\max_{y}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)</annotation></semantics></math>), with corresponding probability denoted as <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A}=\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y_{A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A}=\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y_{A})</annotation></semantics></math>; the maximum probability of the runner-up class is denoted as <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{B}=\max_{y\neq y_{A}}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{B}=\max_{y\neq y_{A}}\mathbb{P}(f(\bm{x}+\bm{\epsilon})=y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Theoretical analysis shows that when an adversarial perturbation <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bm{\tau}\|_{2}&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{\tau}\|_{2}&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, the classification result remains stable (<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i.e." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i.e.</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\bm{x}+\bm{\tau})=y_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝝉</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\bm{x}+\bm{\tau})=y_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>), where the certified radius <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:=\frac{\sigma}{2}\left(\Phi^{-1}(P_{A})-\Phi^{-1}(P_{B})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:=\frac{\sigma}{2}\left(\Phi^{-1}(P_{A})-\Phi^{-1}(P_{B})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse of the standard Gaussian CDF.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, randomized smoothing has been extended to NLP tasks for certified robustness. SAFER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and Text-CRS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> provide certified defenses against adversarial attacks during inference.
CROWD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> applies randomized smoothing to achieve certified robustness against backdoor attacks, aiming to neutralize malicious triggers during training. However, this goal fundamentally conflicts with dataset watermarking, which requires watermark triggers to remain effective throughout training for reliable ownership verification, and we provide a detailed comparison with certified adversarial and backdoor robustness methods in Appendix  <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Comparison with Certified Adversarial and Backdoor Robustness ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Comparison with Certified Adversarial and Backdoor Robustness</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1. </span>Certified Backdoor Robustness</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although both certified backdoor robustness and certified dataset watermarking involve backdoor-like triggers, they pursue fundamentally different objectives. Certified backdoor robustness focuses on ensuring that a model’s predictions remain stable even when poisoned samples appear during training or testing. In this setting, the backdoor is regarded as a harmful perturbation, and the goal is to eliminate or suppress its influence through robust or certified training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pei et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In contrast, certified dataset watermarking deliberately embeds a trigger for legitimate ownership verification. The objective is not to defend against backdoors but to preserve the effect of the trigger under bounded perturbations so that the watermark remains verifiable. Furthermore, existing certified backdoor robustness methods typically depend on modifying the training process, such as applying adversarial or randomized smoothing training to achieve formal guarantees. These approaches assume full control of model training, which is unrealistic in dataset ownership verification scenarios where the adversary or an untrusted party performs the training. Hence, certified backdoor robustness cannot be directly applied to our setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2. </span>Certified Adversarial Robustness</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Certified adversarial robustness and certified dataset watermarking are related in that both aim to provide provable guarantees under bounded perturbations. However, their purposes and perspectives are fundamentally different.
Certified adversarial robustness focuses on ensuring that a model’s prediction remains unchanged within a predefined neighborhood of each input, thereby characterizing the local stability of the decision boundary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In contrast, certified dataset watermarking investigates how a model behaves on watermarked samples that have been intentionally embedded into the training data.
Its objective is to determine whether a model has been trained on a protected dataset by measuring the stability of watermark activation rather than classification consistency. Thus, while adversarial robustness seeks insensitivity to perturbations, certified watermarking relies on persistence of specific trigger effects. Moreover, existing certified adversarial robustness methods rely on perturbation-aware model training or certified smoothing during model optimization.
These methods assume that the defender can modify or retrain the model, which is infeasible in dataset ownership verification scenarios where the suspicious model is already trained by an untrusted party. Therefore, certified adversarial robustness also cannot be directly applied to the DOV problem studied in this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Detailed Experimental Settings</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1. </span>Dataset Watermarking</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and adopt two text backdoor watermarking methods: BadWord <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kurita et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and AddSent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. The target label is fixed as <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, with a watermark rate of 10%. For BadWord, we randomly select 2 triggers from “cf”, “mn”, “bb”, “tq”, “mb” and insert them following distributed or clustered strategies; for AddSent, we use the phrase “I watch this 3D movie” at sentence start, middle, end, or a random position. To constrain stealthiness, <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> perturbations are kept around 0.6 (BadWord) and 1.0 (AddSent) by scaling trigger embeddings with local average pooling over adaptive windows (<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=2</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&lt;10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&lt;10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=10</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 80</annotation></semantics></math>). Token-level permutations are added during training for robustness. For each method–dataset pair, we train 50 watermarked models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2. </span>Dataset Verification</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We reserve 5,000 test samples from AG and SST-2 as verification sets. We train <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=100</annotation></semantics></math> benign models and construct calibration sets with outlier filtering ratios <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> (AG) and <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (SST-2). To estimate false positives, we train 50 independent models on clean data. Verification uses dual-space perturbations: Gaussian noise in the embedding space and local window shuffling in the permutation space. For each test sample, we generate <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1024</annotation></semantics></math> perturbed versions, computing watermark robustness (WR) and principal probability (PP) via Monte Carlo estimation. The significance level <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.05, ensuring 95% confidence under conformal prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>The Analysis of Computational Complexity</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the computational complexity of the proposed DDSmoothing method, focusing on the stages of watermarked dataset generation and ownership verification.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity of Watermarked Dataset Generation.</span>
Let <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denote the size of the text dataset and <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be the poisoning rate. In the generation stage, we only need to embed watermarks into <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\cdot N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\cdot N</annotation></semantics></math> samples, resulting in a complexity of <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\gamma\cdot N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\gamma\cdot N)</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, our method introduces perturbations in both the embedding space and the permutation space: in the embedding space, we optimize trigger embeddings through local pooling and iterative scaling, with a complexity of <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(|P|\cdot u\cdot d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(|P|\cdot u\cdot d)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of trigger positions, <math id="A5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is the window size, and <math id="A5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension; in the permutation space, we perform group-based reordering over a sequence of length <math id="A5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, with a complexity of <math id="A5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since these additional costs are negligible compared with the overall dataset scale, the total complexity can still be regarded as <math id="A5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\gamma\cdot N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\gamma\cdot N)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the extra time overhead is almost negligible in large-scale training pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity of Ownership Verification.</span>
Let <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the numbers of benign models, watermarked models, and independent models, respectively. Training these models requires complexities of <math id="A5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(J)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(W_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(W_{a})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(I_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(I_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and this process can be significantly accelerated through parallel computation. In the verification stage, we need to compute both the watermark robustness (WR) and the principal probability (PP) under dual-space randomized smoothing. For the suspicious model, WR computation involves performing Monte Carlo sampling on watermarked samples from each class while simultaneously injecting noise into both embedding and permutation spaces, which results in a complexity of <math id="A5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(K\cdot M\cdot\mathcal{C}(f))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(K\cdot M\cdot\mathcal{C}(f))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes, <math id="A5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples, and <math id="A5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cost of a single forward pass. For benign models, PP computation also relies on dual-space smoothing and requires estimating prediction distributions across multiple models, with a complexity of <math id="A5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(J\cdot K\cdot M\cdot\mathcal{C}(g))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(J\cdot K\cdot M\cdot\mathcal{C}(g))</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, both WR and PP calculations only involve forward inference and can be highly parallelized on GPUs, making their cost negligible compared with model training. For example, on common text classification datasets, training a benign or watermarked model typically takes several minutes, whereas computing WR and PP under dual-space smoothing can be completed within a few seconds, thus incurring almost no additional overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Results on the AG News Dataset</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we present the results on the AG News dataset in Figure <a href="#A8.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix H Effects of the Number of Benign Models ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. The trends are consistent with those observed on SST-2 in the main paper: while stochastic noise moderately reduces watermark performance, adversarial perturbations are substantially more destructive, further confirming the fundamental vulnerability of existing PLMs dataset watermarking methods under worst-case perturbation scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Additional Effectiveness Results on the AG News Dataset</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we report additional results on the AG News dataset in Figure <a href="#A8.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix H Effects of the Number of Benign Models ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. The results are consistent with those on SST-2, demonstrating that watermarked samples maintain nearly 99% WSR under stochastic noise and remain robust even under worst-case perturbations, indicating that completely removing the watermark becomes significantly more difficult.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Effects of the Number of Benign Models</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#A8.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix H Effects of the Number of Benign Models ‣ DSSmoothing: Toward Certified Dataset Ownership Verification for Pre-trained Language Models via Dual-Space Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, both VSR and WCA increase with the number of benign models <math id="A8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>. On AG, both methods maintain 80–100%, while on SST-2, BadWord’s WCA rises from 28% to 90% and VSR stays above 66%. The VSR of benign models remains around 10%, indicating a low false positive rate. Results show that performance converges when <math id="A8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> reaches 50–100, with limited gains afterward. Thus, dataset owners should choose the number of benign models based on their needs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.15303v1/ag_sst2_benign1.svg" id="A8.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/97;" width="229" height="97"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11. </span>Effects of the number of benign models.</figcaption>
</figure>
